//! English explanations of the side menu's windows (G0.23), by
//! `menu.<window key>`, sorted. The ⓘ modal adds the RouterOS path and the
//! commands itself.


pub(super) const HELP: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("menu.addresses", "## What it does\nThe IPv4 addresses of the router's interfaces. Each address also makes the connected route of its network.\n\nAn address can come from a pool (prefix delegation) or from a DHCP lease; the Origin column says which."),
    ("menu.bgp", "## What it does\nThe BGP instance: the router's AS, router id and the sessions with peers (upstreams, IXs, customers). The protocol itself runs in FRR; this window configures it."),
    ("menu.bonding", "## What it does\nBonds join several ports into one link (LACP 802.3ad, active-backup, balance-xor…) for more capacity and for redundancy when a cable or port fails."),
    ("menu.cgnat", "## What it does\nCarrier-grade NAT for subscribers on shared IPv4 (RFC 6598): deterministic pools (RFC 7422, nothing logged per connection) or port blocks on demand (RFC 6888, one log line per block), 1:1 exceptions, EIM/EIF and hairpin (RFC 4787), \"who was\" (public address + port + time – subscriber), the mapping table, blocks and port alerts."),
    ("menu.dhcp-client", "## What it does\nGets an IPv4 address, gateway and DNS servers from a DHCP server on an interface: the usual WAN of a link that hands out a dynamic address."),
    ("menu.dhcp-relay", "## What it does\nForwards the DHCP requests of a LAN to a central DHCP server in another network (option 82 included), so one server serves many sites."),
    ("menu.dhcp-server", "## What it does\nHands out IPv4 addresses to the hosts of a LAN: servers, networks (gateway, DNS), leases (dynamic and static), options and alerts of rogue servers.\n\nStatistics, history and conflicts of the leases are in the same window. The Statistics tab shows counters and how full each pool is."),
    ("menu.dhcp6-server", "## What it does\nThe DHCPv6 server: hands out IPv6 addresses and delegated prefixes (PD) to customers' routers, and shows the bindings."),
    ("menu.dhcpv6-client", "## What it does\nAsks the upstream for an IPv6 prefix (prefix delegation) and/or an address over DHCPv6, and puts the prefix in a pool for the LANs."),
    ("menu.dns", "## What it does\nThe router's DNS resolver: upstream servers, DNS over HTTPS/TLS, whether the LAN may ask it (allow remote requests), cache size and limits per client."),
    ("menu.dns-adlist", "## What it does\nBlock lists of domains (ads, malware) loaded from a URL or a file: a name on a list gets no answer, like a Pi-hole in the router."),
    ("menu.dns-cache", "## What it does\nWhat the resolver has in its cache now: names, records, TTL and hits. Flush empties it."),
    ("menu.dns-forwarders", "## What it does\nNamed groups of DNS servers (plain or DoH/DoT) that static FWD entries send some names to: conditional forwarding of an internal domain, for example."),
    ("menu.dns-lookup", "## What it does\nAsks a name (or the PTR of an address) to the router's resolver or to a server you choose, and shows the records and how long it took. Like nslookup or dig."),
    ("menu.dns-static", "## What it does\nNames the router answers itself (A, AAAA, CNAME, MX, TXT…), forwards elsewhere (FWD) or blocks (NXDOMAIN); a regexp matches many names at once."),
    ("menu.dns-statistics", "## What it does\nCounters of the resolver: queries by protocol and type, where the answers came from (cache, static, upstream), response codes, top clients and the upstream servers' latency."),
    ("menu.dns-tsig", "## What it does\nTSIG keys (RFC 8945): shared secrets that sign zone transfers and dynamic updates between DNS servers."),
    ("menu.dns-zones", "## What it does\nZones the router is authoritative for: primary or secondary, transfers, DNSSEC and dynamic updates.\n\nThree tabs: **Zones**, the **Records** of a zone (SOA, NS, A, MX…) and the **Zone File** in the standard (BIND) format, to export or import."),
    ("menu.firewall", "## What it does\nPacket filtering and translation: filter rules (what passes), NAT (masquerade, port forwarding), mangle, address lists and zones.\n\nA rule that would cut your own access is held and explained before it applies."),
    ("menu.history", "## What it does\nEvery committed change of the configuration, who made it and when. Pick a revision to see the difference or to go back to it (rollback)."),
    ("menu.identity", "## What it does\nThe router's name: shown in the terminal prompt, in the neighbors' lists and in the logs."),
    ("menu.interface-list", "## What it does\nNamed groups of interfaces (LAN, WAN…) that firewall rules, DNS and other features name instead of each port."),
    ("menu.interfaces", "## What it does\nEvery interface of the router: ports, VLANs, bridges, bonds and loopbacks, with their state, MTU and traffic. Double-click one for its details."),
    ("menu.ip-calc", "## What it does\nIP calculator: network, broadcast, hosts, mask and wildcard of an address (IPv4 and IPv6), split a network, summarize prefixes, range to prefixes and the overlap warning against the configuration; how many IPv6 delegations (PD) a prefix holds; and the CGNAT planner, with the pools' own formula (RFC 7422): what fits, what to change, the mapping preview, create the pool, export."),
    ("menu.ip-pool", "## What it does\nRanges of IPv4 addresses that the DHCP server (and later PPP and CGNAT) hand out, and which ones are in use."),
    ("menu.ipv6-pool", "## What it does\nIPv6 prefixes to split into smaller ones: the prefix the upstream delegated (DHCPv6 client) becomes the /64s of the LANs."),
    ("menu.ipv6-settings", "## What it does\nGlobal IPv6 switches: forwarding between interfaces and whether router advertisements are accepted (SLAAC on the WAN)."),
    ("menu.log", "## What it does\nThe router's messages as they happen, by topic and severity, with search."),
    ("menu.logging", "## What it does\nWhere the messages go: rules by topic and actions (memory, disk, remote syslog)."),
    ("menu.mpls", "## What it does\nMPLS label switching and LDP, for VPLS and L3VPN between sites. Not implemented yet."),
    ("menu.mtr", "## What it does\nTraceroute that keeps going: each hop of the path with loss and latency over time, AS and operator of each hop. Shows where along the path a problem starts."),
    ("menu.neighbors", "## What it does\nDevices seen on the links by LLDP and the router's own discovery: name, address, platform and the port they are on."),
    ("menu.ntp-client", "## What it does\nKeeps the router's clock right from NTP servers. Logs, certificates and DHCP leases depend on the time being correct."),
    ("menu.ospf", "## What it does\nOSPF, the interior routing protocol: areas, interfaces and neighbors. Not implemented yet."),
    ("menu.ping", "## What it does\nSends ICMP echo to an address and charts the latency and loss; also finds the path MTU."),
    ("menu.routes", "## What it does\nThe routing table: static routes (the default route 0.0.0.0/0 included), connected and learned ones, which one is active and in which VRF."),
    ("menu.services", "## What it does\nThe router's own services (DNS, DHCP, metrics…) as the supervisor sees them: running, stopped or failed, restarts and the last error. Start, stop and restart them here."),
    ("menu.settings", "## What it does\nPreferences of this GUI, not of the router: the language of the labels and of the explanations. Kept on this computer."),
    ("menu.tuning", "## What it does\nKernel and hardware tuning for routing: network buffers, conntrack, CPU governor, IRQ and RPS/XPS per interface, and the boot parameters — with recommended values and why."),
    ("menu.vrf", "## What it does\nVRFs: separate routing tables on the same router, for customers or management kept apart."),
];
